    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d78">

                <pb facs="0158" n="74" sameAs="frus:frus1903/pg74"/>

                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Conger to Mr. Hay.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Legation of the United States</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Peking</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1903-08-25"><hi rendition="#italic">August 25,
                        1903</hi>.</date></dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 1374.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: A British officer, just returned from the
                    mouth of the Yalu River, reports most unfavorably as to the advisability of
                    opening Ta-tung-kou as a treaty port, and says that An-tung Hsien, sometimes
                    called Saho, some 30 miles farther inland, is much more suitable, having a
                    greater depth of water and being nearer the crossing of the principal route from
                    Manchuria into Korea.</p>
                <p>Ta-tung-kou was selected from the best evidence then obtainable. (See my
                    dispatch, No. 1252, of March 31, last.)</p>
                <p>As it will be impossible to know the facts without an intelligent examination of
                    both places, I have requested Admiral Evans to send one of his smaller vessels
                    to the mouth of the Yalu and then, by steam launch or otherwise, make the
                    necessary exploration and report.</p>
                <p>I have, etc.,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">E. H. Conger.</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
